{
  "name": "How to Plan a More Functional Kitchen Layout",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Getting a kitchen layout that truly works for your family involves a bit of thoughtful planning. You want a space that's not just pretty, but super efficient for how you live and cook. Here's a straightforward approach to help you get there:",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 2",
      "text": "**Step 1: Observe Your Current Habits.** Before you change anything, spend a few days really noticing how your family uses the kitchen. What's frustrating? What works well? How many people cook at once? Where do you prep food, store dishes, and put your trash? Understanding your real-world use will guide every decision.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 2
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 3",
      "text": "**Step 2: Define Your Work Zones.** Think about your kitchen in terms of zones: a prep zone, a cooking zone, a cleaning zone, and a storage zone. Each should have appropriate counter space, storage, and access to appliances. Make sure your dishwasher is close to your sink and main prep area to streamline cleanup.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 3
    },
    {
      "name": "Step 4",
      "text": "**Step 3: Plan for Circulation and Clearances.** You need enough space to move around comfortably, even when appliance doors are open. The path between your fridge, sink, and stove (the classic",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 4
    }
  ],
